#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129945} :activity/id #uuid "b7bdf46c-999f-51d6-8068-e087fa71c2e3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049759 :topic/id #uuid "1184fca5-883d-500f-a017-8e5965abd89d" :topic/math-academy-id 2013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130341 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to divide one polynomial by another. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's introduce some terminology:\n\n- the ** dividend** is the polynomial that we want to divide ($2x^2+8x-5$)\n\n- the **divisor** is the polynomial by which we want to divide the dividend ($x+5$)\n\n- the result of the division is the **quotient**\n\n- the **remainder** is what is left after the division\n\nTo carry out this division, we will use the method of **long division of polynomials**. \n\n**Note:** Up to now, you may have used synthetic division for this kind of task. However, you should still learn the long division method. As we'll soon see, long division allows us to divide any two polynomials, whereas synthetic division only works if the divisor is a linear binomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "f5dd6a03-4dcd-5620-b917-0c2188b20fd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3323 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1409e5d1-8f3c-5a63-819c-c4a57f9ccb08" :step/math-academy-id 18478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129117 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128930 :question/id #uuid "2b2f2b8e-4625-5550-bb37-5ab9ca409c50" :question/math-academy-id "e-5032"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd4abd5d-2642-5f0d-af2f-5ed5c265526a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476955}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial"} :step/id #uuid "fc98e15b-f0c5-5510-b41a-5feef4ed5c9d" :step/math-academy-id 18479 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130342 :tutorial/content "When we divide a dividend by a divisor, we get a quotient and a remainder.\n\nIn general, we can write the result of any division as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{dividend}}{\\text{divisor}}={\\color{blue}\\text{quotient}} +\\dfrac{{\\color{red}\\text{remainder}}}{\\text{divisor}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0dc46f89-ba15-57ca-a9f5-29a2c3f43413" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3324 :tutorial/title "Writing the Result of a Polynomial Division"} :step/id #uuid "8b344aae-f200-5146-aeb2-98fc8d8e0d34" :step/math-academy-id 18480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130343 :tutorial/content "To check that a polynomial division result is correct, we can multiply both sides of the equation by the divisor and ensure that both sides are equal.\n\nFor example, let's check that the following polynomial division result is correct.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x^2+8x - 5}{x+5}=2x-2 +\\dfrac{5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the above by $(x+5)$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\times (\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x-5}{x+5}) & =(x+5)\\times (2x-2+\\frac{5}{x+5}) \\\\ \\frac{(2{x}^{2}+8x-5)(x+5)}{x+5} & =(2x-2)(x+5)+\\frac{5(x+5)}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{(2{x}^{2}+8x-5)\\cancel{(x+5)}}{\\cancel{x+5}} & =(2x-2)(x+5)+\\frac{5\\cancel{(x+5)}}{\\cancel{x+5}} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =(2x-2)(x+5)+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the parentheses on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =(2x-2)(x+5)+5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =2{x}^{2}+10x-2x-10+5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =2{x}^{2}+8x-5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement. Therefore, the result is indeed correct." :tutorial/id #uuid "9bc4e2a8-e136-5730-ba7d-1ee08a571479" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3325 :tutorial/title "Checking a Polynomial Division Result"} :step/id #uuid "da83aca8-147b-572d-b57f-ed106f195009" :step/math-academy-id 18481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130344 :tutorial/content "When using polynomial division, we must write both the dividend and divisor in descending powers of $x.$ \n\nIf there are missing powers of $x,$ then we need to introduce these terms using zero coefficients.\n\nFor example, consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2+5}{x+1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the dividend has no $x$ term. To use polynomial division, we need to fill in the missing term using a coefficient of zero. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2+{\\color{blue} 0x}+5}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe polynomial in the dividend now contains every term in the correct order with nothing missing." :tutorial/id #uuid "95f14b28-71e8-5d64-817e-7253f2452ac8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3326 :tutorial/title "Dealing With Missing Terms in the Dividend"} :step/id #uuid "6640982a-0850-5d23-933d-79f713fd3c80" :step/math-academy-id 18482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129118 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128931 :question/id #uuid "bda2e91a-5b55-5deb-8232-7e79fabf6f29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5033"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c342f1-881b-5f3e-8c76-448eec165168" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476971}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial With Some Zero Coefficients by a Linear Binomial"} :step/id #uuid "38eb2f39-74b3-50fc-b708-e4db170e6bfb" :step/math-academy-id 18483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129120 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128933 :question/id #uuid "e3e171d1-4428-5a4d-a050-5ad8eb843aff" :question/math-academy-id "e-5110"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bdfd8fc-ce8d-5950-870d-c0570ef755bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476935}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Result of Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial"} :step/id #uuid "4b8008ef-b02a-5f48-a9e7-8abd9c065f1f" :step/math-academy-id 18751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130415 :tutorial/content "Our task is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In this case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to begin.\n\nTo carry out the division, we follow the following step-by-step process.\n\n **Step 1** \n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n **Step 2** \n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}2x^2})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}2x^2}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}2x}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}{({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)}={2x^2+10x}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract ${2x^2+10x}$ from ${2x^2+8x - 5}$ and get ${{-2x-5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3** \n\nWe now repeat the process. The dividend is now ${{\\color{red}{-2x}}-5}.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}-2x})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}-2x}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}$ term we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}({{\\color{blue}{x}}+5})={-2x-10}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract ${{-2x-10}}$ from ${{-2x-5}}$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant ${\\color{red}{5}}.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is $\\color{blue}{2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0ac468d3-641b-5a89-898e-f629ecf34ab7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5602 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "608687e4-4c41-5794-bd39-17d55ba92545" :step/math-academy-id 34514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129946}}] :activity/title "Dividing Polynomials by Linear Binomials Using Long Division" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129356}]}
